[2026-09-16T19:04:47] Q074: Describe how Spielberg came to make Schindler's List and how it affected his later work.
{"id": "Q074", "question": "Describe how Spielberg came to make Schindler's List and how it affected his later work.", "information_need": "in_depth", "motivation": "intrinsic_learning", "prior_familiarity": "unfamiliar", "scope": "multi_document", "answerable": true, "adversarial_kind": null, "required_sources": ["Steven_Spielberg.html"], "expected_sources": ["Steven_Spielberg.html", "Academy_Award_for_Best_Director.html", "Schindler_s_List.html"], "retrieved_sources": ["Schindler_s_List.html", "Steven_Spielberg.html", "Saving_Private_Ryan.html"], "required_aspects": ["Spielberg waited ten years to make Schindler's List because he did not feel mature enough, wanting to embrace his heritage", "after the birth of his son Max, he said a spirit ignited in him and he became 'a Jewish dad'.", "Principal photography began March 1, 1993 in Kraków, Poland, while he supervised post-production on Jurassic Park", "he brought his wife and children to make the shoot bearable.", "The film was a commercial success and Spielberg used his percentage of the profits to start the Shoah Foundation, a non-profit archiving Holocaust survivor testimonies.", "It won seven Academy Awards, including Best Picture and his first Best Director."], "response": "Spielberg became interested in making *Schindler’s List* after MCA president Sid Sheinberg sent him a New York Times review of *Schindler’s Ark* [Schindler_s_List.html]. Universal bought the rights, but Spielberg was unsure he was ready to make a Holocaust film and tried to pass the project to other directors before deciding to direct it himself [Schindler_s_List.html]. He was finally motivated to take it on when he saw Holocaust deniers being taken seriously and worried about rising neo-Nazism and growing tolerance for intolerance [Schindler_s_List.html]. The film was made on a $22 million budget, and Spielberg took no salary, calling it “blood money” [Schindler_s_List.html].\n\nThe film had a major impact on his later work and career. Following its success, Spielberg founded the Survivors of the Shoah Visual History Foundation to preserve Holocaust survivor testimony, and he used proceeds from the film to finance related documentaries [Schindler_s_List.html]. It also marked a major turn in his filmmaking toward more serious dramatic work; in 1993 he went on to direct *Schindler’s List* after *Jurassic Park*, and the biography notes that he “won the Academy Award for Best Director” for it [Steven_Spielberg.html]. The documents also connect the film to his personal life and Jewish identity: Spielberg said he wanted to “embrace his heritage” while making it [Steven_Spielberg.html], and the experience was deeply emotional for him [Schindler_s_List.html].", "closed_book_response": "Steven Spielberg came to make *Schindler’s List* after years of being drawn to the subject but feeling uncertain he was the right person to tell it. The project had circulated in Hollywood in various forms, and Spielberg was interested in producing it, but he hesitated to direct because the material was so grave and personal. He was also aware that, as a Jewish filmmaker with a huge commercial profile, he would be judged very closely on how he handled the Holocaust. Eventually, after other versions and approaches stalled, he decided to take it on himself, in part because he felt a responsibility to tell the story and because he had matured enough as a filmmaker to approach it with the restraint it required.\n\nMaking the film was a major turning point in his career. It shifted him from being seen primarily as a blockbuster entertainer to being recognized as a filmmaker capable of profound historical and emotional seriousness. The experience also deeply affected him personally. It led him to become more involved in Holocaust remembrance and Jewish causes, and it seems to have made his later work more attentive to history, trauma, and human suffering. While he continued to make large-scale popular films, he also increasingly pursued prestige dramas and historical subjects, and *Schindler’s List* became one of the defining works that broadened how audiences and critics understood him.", "correctness": "pass", "graded_correctness": "complete", "graded_correctness_score": 1.0, "covered_aspects": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], "aspect_coverage": 1.0, "answer_relevance": "pass", "claims": [{"text": "Steven Spielberg became interested in making Schindler's List after MCA president Sid Sheinberg sent him a New York Times review of Schindler's Ark.", "cited_source": "Schindler_s_List.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "Universal bought the rights to the novel.", "cited_source": "Schindler_s_List.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "Spielberg was unsure he was ready to make a Holocaust film.", "cited_source": "Schindler_s_List.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "He tried to pass the project to other directors before deciding to direct it himself.", "cited_source": "Schindler_s_List.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "He was finally motivated to take on the film when he saw Holocaust deniers being taken seriously and worried about rising neo-Nazism and growing tolerance for intolerance.", "cited_source": "Schindler_s_List.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "The film was made on a $22 million budget.", "cited_source": "Schindler_s_List.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "Spielberg took no salary for the film.", "cited_source": "Schindler_s_List.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "Spielberg called his unpaid salary 'blood money'.", "cited_source": "Schindler_s_List.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "Following the film's success, Spielberg founded the Survivors of the Shoah Visual History Foundation to preserve Holocaust survivor testimony.", "cited_source": "Schindler_s_List.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "He used proceeds from the film to finance related documentaries.", "cited_source": "Schindler_s_List.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "The film marked a major turn in Spielberg's filmmaking toward more serious dramatic work.", "cited_source": "Steven_Spielberg.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "In 1993 Spielberg went on to direct Schindler's List after Jurassic Park.", "cited_source": "Steven_Spielberg.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": false}, {"text": "Spielberg won the Academy Award for Best Director for Schindler's List.", "cited_source": "Steven_Spielberg.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": false}, {"text": "Spielberg said he wanted to 'embrace his heritage' while making the film.", "cited_source": "Steven_Spielberg.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "The experience of making the film was deeply emotional for Spielberg.", "cited_source": "Schindler_s_List.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": false}], "claim_count": 15, "graded_groundedness_score": 1.0, "faithfulness": "pass", "citation_support_rate": 1.0, "chunk_attribution_score": 0.8, "source_hit_at_k": 1, "source_recall_at_k": 1.0, "source_precision_at_k": 0.6666666666666666, "source_recall_strict": 1.0, "all_required_present": true, "response_quote_count": 3, "quote_fidelity_rate": 1.0, "quote_attribution_accuracy": 0.6666666666