[2026-09-16T19:05:23] Q094: I'm deciding whether to watch a long Western — how does Costner's Horizon project compare in scale and reception to his earlier epic?
{"id": "Q094", "question": "I'm deciding whether to watch a long Western — how does Costner's Horizon project compare in scale and reception to his earlier epic?", "information_need": "in_depth", "motivation": "personal_decision", "prior_familiarity": "familiar", "scope": "multi_document", "answerable": true, "adversarial_kind": null, "required_sources": ["Kevin_Costner.html"], "expected_sources": ["Kevin_Costner.html", "Academy_Award_for_Best_Director.html"], "retrieved_sources": ["Kevin_Costner.html", "Clint_Eastwood.html", "Christopher_Nolan.html"], "required_aspects": ["Horizon: An American Saga is a Western epic split into at least four films, each just under three hours long, with Costner both directing and starring", "production on the first film was expected to last at least 220 days.", "Chapter 1 was released in June 2024, earned mixed reviews and was a box office flop, which contributed to Chapter 2 being delayed indefinitely after debuting at the 81st Venice International Film Festival to similarly mixed reviews.", "By contrast, his earlier epic Dances With Wolves won him two Academy Awards, and his 2003 Western Open Range received critical acclaim and was a surprise commercial success."], "response": "Costner’s *Horizon: An American Saga* is described as a Western epic that “will be split into at least four films, each just under three hours in length,” with production beginning in August 2022 and the first film released in June 2024 [Kevin_Costner.html]. The project was planned as a series of months-long releases, and the first film’s production was expected to last at least 220 days [Kevin_Costner.html].\n\nBy comparison, *Dances With Wolves* (1990) was a single epic film; it was nominated for 12 Academy Awards and won seven, including Best Picture and Best Director for Costner [Kevin_Costner.html]. So in scale, *Horizon* is larger as a multi-film project, while *Dances With Wolves* was a standalone epic with major awards success [Kevin_Costner.html].\n\nIn reception, *Horizon: An American Saga – Chapter 1* “earned mixed reviews” and “was a box office flop,” and the poor box-office returns were a factor in the indefinite delay of Chapter 2, which also “had received mixed reviews” [Kevin_Costner.html]. *Dances With Wolves*, by contrast, won major Academy Awards and established a much more celebrated reputation [Kevin_Costner.html].", "closed_book_response": "Kevin Costner’s **Horizon** project is ambitious in a way that’s very comparable to his earlier Western epic, **Dances with Wolves**, but the two projects differ a lot in **scale of production, rollout, and reception**.\n\n### Scale\n- **Dances with Wolves (1990)** was a single film, relatively traditional in form, though huge by Western standards at the time.\n- **Horizon** is much more sprawling: it’s designed as a **multi-part saga** (Costner has described it as several chapters), with a much larger narrative canvas and a more serial, “event” structure.\n\nSo in terms of **scope**, Horizon is the bigger and more unusually ambitious project.\n\n### Reception\n- **Dances with Wolves** was a major critical and commercial success. It won multiple Oscars, including **Best Picture** and **Best Director** for Costner, and it was widely praised as a revitalization of the Western.\n- **Horizon** has had a much rougher start. The first chapter got a **mixed-to-negative critical response** and, at least initially, weak box-office results. The overall reception has been more divided, with many people admiring the ambition but questioning the storytelling and pacing.\n\n### Bottom line\nIf you’re comparing them:\n- **Horizon is larger in scale**\n- **Dances with Wolves is much more celebrated and better received**\n\nSo if you’re choosing based on reputation alone, **Dances with Wolves has the stronger track record**. If you want a sprawling, contemporary Western experiment and don’t mind unevenness, **Horizon** is the more audacious watch.", "correctness": "pass", "graded_correctness": "complete", "graded_correctness_score": 1.0, "covered_aspects": [1, 2, 3, 4], "aspect_coverage": 1.0, "answer_relevance": "pass", "claims": [{"text": "Costner’s *Horizon: An American Saga* is described as a Western epic that will be split into at least four films, each just under three hours in length.", "cited_source": "Kevin_Costner.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "Production on *Horizon* began in August 2022.", "cited_source": "Kevin_Costner.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "The first *Horizon* film was released in June 2024.", "cited_source": "Kevin_Costner.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "The project was planned as a series of months-long releases.", "cited_source": "Kevin_Costner.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "Production on the first *Horizon* film was expected to last at least 220 days.", "cited_source": "Kevin_Costner.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "*Dances With Wolves* (1990) was a single epic film.", "cited_source": "Kevin_Costner.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": false}, {"text": "*Dances With Wolves* was nominated for 12 Academy Awards.", "cited_source": "Kevin_Costner.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": false}, {"text": "*Dances With Wolves* won seven Academy Awards, including Best Picture and Best Director for Costner.", "cited_source": "Kevin_Costner.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": false}, {"text": "In scale, *Horizon* is larger as a multi-film project.", "cited_source": "Kevin_Costner.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": false, "attributable_to_retrieval":